<speak>
<p>Let u be odd and greater than one. The all-ones polynomial J has u squared terms. Substitute P equal to one: every power becomes one, so J of one equals u squared. For u equal to three, count nine ones before taking any residue.<break time="700ms"/>Now use the same nonzero characteristic-two quotient ring as before. Two equals the product of one plus i and one minus i, so a pair of ones reduces to zero. In the residue calculation, pair eight of the nine ones; one remains. In general, u squared is odd, so J of one has residue one modulo one plus i.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
<p>Our sum contains J of one divided by c of one, so account for the denominator too. The polynomial c has real integer coefficients and satisfies c times c star equals u squared. Star conjugates the coefficients and reverses the powers. At P equal to one, every reversed power is still one, and conjugating a real coefficient leaves it unchanged. Thus c star of one equals c of one.<break time="700ms"/>The evaluated norm identity becomes c of one squared equals u squared. Therefore c of one is either u or negative u. The real-coefficient condition restricts the value to these two real possibilities.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
<p>Use the evaluation ring with one over u adjoined. The denominator c of one, equal to positive or negative u, is a unit there. Simplify the exact quotient before reducing: J of one over c of one equals u squared over positive or negative u, giving positive or negative u.<break time="700ms"/>For u equal to three, nine divided by three is three, and nine divided by negative three is negative three. Now take residues. Since u is odd, its residue is one. Plus and minus agree in characteristic two, so either sign of the quotient has residue one.<break time="700ms"/>The empty subset has input P equal to one and positive alternating sign. Its normalized contribution, including the unit denominator, therefore has residue one in the same common ring.<break time="1200ms"/></p>
</speak>
